{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788225829,"nonce":"PhJQcatCjuGPB341Jy2LD/ylUA0=","title":"Base composition: a 7x7 module grid, so layouts tile instead of landing as islands","message":"Design decision by Evan, session 11 (`65b03c2`), 2026-08-31, in his own framing:\n\n\u003e \"separate rooms would be the easiest but a base is usually connected rooms, a\n\u003e grid would be the easiest, people usually make 7x7 rooms and then they can\n\u003e section them off into quarters, halves, or corner shapes, it's a really\n\u003e efficient way to make a base\"\n\n\n3.3 (`1adc737`) specifies `place-layout {file.ir.json} --origin P`. It places ONE\nlayout at ONE origin and takes no position on how layouts relate to each other.\nThere is no composition rule anywhere in the spec or the corpus — so an agent\nfollowing it builds **disconnected islands**, which is not what a base is.\n\nThe corpus confirms the gap rather than just implying it. The three shipped\ntemplates have footprints **5x7, 11x6 and 7x7** (`templates/INDEX.md`). No two of\nthem tile. There is no shared module, so even placing them adjacent produces\nragged seams and double walls.\n\n`power-room` is ALREADY 7x7, so the module is not a new invention — it is the one\nthe corpus half-adopted without saying so.\n\n\n**7x7 is the module.** A room is one cell of the grid, or a subdivision of one:\nquarters, halves, or a corner shape. Rooms connect; the grid is what makes them\nconnect predictably.\n\n\n- **Shared walls become the norm.** One wall belongs to two rooms. Anything that\n  assumes a room owns its own perimeter — enclosure checks, the roof pass, the\n  material bill — has to stop assuming it, or every interior wall gets built and\n  billed twice.\n- **It makes `acee526` (1.9) LOAD-BEARING, not optional.** Placement is not\n  exact-or-refuse today: things slide silently to a nearby cell. On a free-standing\n  hut a one-cell slide is cosmetic. **On a grid it is cumulative** — one slid wall\n  puts every subsequent module out of phase and the base stops being a grid, with\n  no error anywhere saying so. Note the known hazard on that issue: session 9 found\n  the proposed fix route currently DELETES buildings already in the target cell.\n  So 1.9 must be fixed properly before a grid can be trusted, and 3.3 already\n  depends on it.\n- 3.3's acceptance rehearsal is a **5x7** bedroom. Either that becomes 7x7 (or a\n  subdivision of one), or the rehearsal deliberately tests the non-modular path\n  and says so.\n\n\n- A module size (7x7) recorded in DESIGN.md as a decision, with this reasoning.\n- A grid origin/phase concept: layouts declare which module cell(s) they occupy,\n  so `place-layout` can refuse an origin that is off-phase rather than silently\n  placing a base that will not tile.\n- Subdivision vocabulary: quarter, half, corner — as IR the templates can express,\n  not as prose.\n- Shared-wall semantics: who owns an interior wall, and how the material bill\n  avoids counting it twice.\n\n\n- `templates/*.ir.json` and `templates/INDEX.md` — the three current footprints\n  and the format's two halves.\n- `rimworld-tools/baseviz/ir.py` — the IR dialect, `size`, `layers`. Note\n  INDEX.md's open point that \"row 0 = north is PROPOSED, not established — 3.3\n  pins it\"; a grid makes that pin urgent, because phase is meaningless without a\n  fixed orientation.\n- `1adc737` (3.3) — `place-layout`, preflight-every-cell, blueprint vs instant.\n- `acee526` (1.9) — exact-or-refuse placement, and its deletion hazard.\n\n\n- The module size and subdivision vocabulary are in DESIGN.md's decisions log\n  with the reasoning above.\n- A layout declares its module footprint, and `place-layout` REFUSES an\n  off-phase origin with a reason naming the phase it expected — it does not\n  place and hope.\n- Two adjacent modules placed in sequence share their common wall: it is built\n  once, billed once, and both rooms read as enclosed.\n- The three existing templates are either re-cut to the module or explicitly\n  marked non-modular in `INDEX.md`, so the corpus stops being silently ragged.\n- A rehearsal places at least a 2x2 block of modules and the result is a\n  connected base, not four huts.","files":null}]}